==Old Norse==
{{wikipedia|Fylgja}}

===Etymology 1===
Common Germanic ''*fulgjō-'' "guardian spirit"

====Noun====
{{non-noun|f}}

# [[companionship]]
# kind of [[guardian angel]], guardian spirit, tutelary entity, which was held to follow each person or family and the relationship being affixed or bound at the process or ceremony of naming.

===Etymology 2===
From {{etyl|gem-pro|non}} {{term/t|gem-pro|*fulgijaną||to follow}}, whence Old English {{term/t|ang|folġian}}, {{term|fylgan|lang=ang}} (English {{term|follow|lang=en}}), Old Frisian {{term|folgia|lang=ofs}} (West Frisian {{term|folgje|lang=fy}}), Old Saxon {{term|fulgon|lang=osx}}, Dutch {{term|volgen|lang=nl}}, Old High German {{term/t|goh|folgēn}} (German {{term|folgen|lang=de}}).

====Verb====
{{non-verb}}

# to [[accompany]], to [[follow]]

====Descendants====
* Danish: {{l|da|følge}}
* Faroese: {{l|is|fylgja}}
* Icelandic: {{l|is|fylgja}}
* Norwegian: {{l|no|følge}}
* Swedish: {{l|sv|följa}}

